    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d254">

                <pb facs="0429" n="301" sameAs="frus:frus1928v01/pg301"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A16/51: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Representative on the Special
                        Commission</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-08-27T12:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 27,
                                1928—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received August 27—9:12
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1. Article V of your draft of treaty discussing category III appears to limit
                    “civil aircraft” to those “manufactured under military specifications.”
                    Examination of Traffic in Arms Convention discloses no such limitation and I
                    would appreciate analysis of Department’s views in this matter. I have requested
                        Yount<anchor n="59" xml:id="d254-f1"/> to proceed from Paris to advise me on
                    this and other questions.</p>
                <p>I assume that full publicity concerning aircraft is made by our Government
                    departments. If this is so could we not safely promise full publicity on “civil
                    aviation” without limitation?</p>
                <p>Address Ammission.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d254-f1" n="59" xml:id="d254-f1-content">
                <p>Major Burton K. Yount, assistant military attaché for air, American Embassy,
                    Paris.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
